	<section aria-labelledby="title"><p class="eyebrow">Let the color travel. Let the letters keep it.</p><h1 id="title">What <em>stays.</em></h1><p class="intro">The wash can cross every letter and all the space around it.<br />Clear the wash, and the adhesive type keeps an impression.</p><AdhesivePigmentSurface /></section>
	<aside><p>The word is no longer the vessel.<br /><em>It is the place that remembers.</em></p><details><summary>What is being demonstrated?</summary><p>Mobile pigment moves over a whole, traversable 192 × 96 sheet. Glyph coverage supplies finite attachment capacity, not internal walls. Pointer movement transfers nearby attached pigment back into the mobile state and drives conservative, bounded transport. An explicit clearing sink removes mobile pigment outside adhesive areas, with a separate per-color ledger.</p><p>“Clear wash” applies the same bounded local reaction as a direct still-state control. It does not secretly run hundreds of simulation frames. It works with reduced motion. The optical pigment mixture and six-second reaction illustration are art-directed, not measured chemistry, water evaporation, or a solved capillary free surface.</p><p>The glyph source and output use actual native pixels, including fractional layout and 3× DPR; resizing never changes the transport grid or the mass ledger. Compare this with <a href="/lab/interfaces/capillary-type">the sealed-letter experiment</a>, whose letters really are containing walls.</p></details></aside>
